{"id":"/page/7/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h2>Modernising the Economy</h2>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Another important part of the Meiji reforms was the modernising of the economy. Funds were raised by levying an agricultural tax. Japan's first railway line, between Tokyo and the port of Yokohama, was built in 1870-72. Textile machinery was imported from Europe, and foreign technicians were employed to train workers, as well as to teach in universities and schools, and Japanese students were sent abroad. In 1872, modern banking institutions were launched. Companies like Mitsubishi and Sumitomo were helped through subsidies and tax benefits to become major shipbuilders so that Japanese trade was from now on carried in Japanese ships. <i>Zaibatsu</i> (large business organisations controlled by individual families) dominated the economy till after the Second World War.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The population, 35 million in 1872, increased to 55 million in 1920. To reduce population pressure the government actively encouraged migration, first to the northern island of Hokkaido, which had been a largely autonomous area where the indigenous people called the Ainu lived, and then to Hawaii and Brazil, as well as to the growing colonial empire of Japan. Within Japan there was a shift to towns as industry developed. By 1925, 21 per cent of the population lived in cities; by 1935, this figure had gone up to 32 per cent (22.5 million).</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/SectionHeader/4","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>Industrial Workers</h3>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The number of people in manufacturing increased from 700,000 in 1870 to 4 million in 1913. Most of them worked in units employing less than five people and using neither machinery nor electric power.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Caption/6","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Workers in a textile factory.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Picture/7","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Interior of a large textile factory with rows of spinning machines and workers.\" src=\"f01b22e5bb303653828200595aece414_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a vast industrial textile mill with multiple rows of spinning frames loaded with spools of thread, supported by tall pillars, with two workers standing in the foreground.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Interior of a large textile factory with rows of spinning machines and workers.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The photograph shows a vast textile mill filled with tightly packed rows of spinning machines carrying numerous spools and vertical spindles, extending from the foreground into the distance. Tall pillars support the ceiling, with belts, shafts, and pulleys running overhead. Two workers stand with their backs to the camera beside the nearest machine, one at either side of the foreground. The repeated rows emphasize the scale of textile production and the machinery surrounding the workers; there are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Over half of those employed in modern factories were women. And it was women who organised the first modern strike in 1886. After 1900, the number of men began to increase but only in the 1930s did male workers begin to outnumber women.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/9","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The size of factories also began to increase. Factories employing more than a hundred workers, just over 1,000 in 1909, jumped to over 2,000 by 1920 and 4,000 by the 1930s; yet even in 1940, there were over 550,000 workshops</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/7/PageFooter/10","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>that employed less than five employees. This sustained the family-centred ideology, just as nationalism was sustained by a strong patriarchal system under an emperor who was like a family patriarch.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The rapid and unregulated growth of industry and the demand for natural resources such as timber led to environmental destruction. Tanaka Shozo, elected to the first House of Representatives, launched the first agitation against industrial pollution in 1897 with 800 villagers in a mass protest forcing the government to take action.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>Aggressive Nationalism</h3>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The Meiji constitution was based on a restricted franchise and created a Diet (the Japanese used the German word for parliament because of the influence of German legal ideas) with limited powers. The leaders who brought about the imperial restoration continued to exercise power and even established political parties. Between 1918 and 1931, popularly elected prime ministers formed cabinets. Thereafter, they lost power to national unity cabinets formed across party lines. The emperor was the commander of the forces and from 1890 this was interpreted to mean that the army and the navy had independent control. In 1899, the prime minister ordered that only serving generals and admirals could become ministers. This strengthening of the military, together with the expansion of Japan's colonial empire, was connected with the fear that Japan was at the mercy of the Western powers. This fear was used to silence opposition to military expansion and to higher taxes to fund the armed forces.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/5","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Illustration of the cover of 'Shimin no Yu' (Friend of the Nation) magazine, showing three children in a row, arms outstretched, with the title '少國民の友' at the top.\" src=\"aaec7c7044d764c324cdb35c0201da6f_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">Illustration of the cover of 'Shimin no Yu' (Friend of the Nation) magazine, showing three children in a row, arms outstretched, with the title '少國民の友' at the top.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure shows a tilted, yellow-orange magazine cover with large red Japanese characters across the top and smaller dark characters on the right. Three young people, wearing headbands and with their upper bodies bare, stand in a staggered formation with their arms extended straight forward at shoulder height; the two in front wear dark trousers or shorts with belts. A broad blue-green band runs behind them across the middle of the cover. The coordinated pose presents young people participating in disciplined physical training, conveying a message of collective readiness.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/6","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Soldiers in uniform marching in a line carrying rifles.\" src=\"3e513f3d2635342934cf8ad226cfa879_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a line of uniformed soldiers marching forward on a wet, reflective surface, each holding a rifle upright.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Soldiers in uniform marching in a line carrying rifles.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The black-and-white photograph shows rows of young uniformed soldiers marching toward the camera on a wet, reflective street. They wear peaked caps and carry long rifles upright at their shoulders, with the barrels and bayonets extending above their heads. The repeated ranks and similar uniforms emphasize collective military discipline; there are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/7","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Bandaged lower legs and feet of a person standing outdoors.\" src=\"f5aaecb6473222e3d65f65164fab048e_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows the lower legs and feet of a person wrapped in multiple layers of bandages, wearing boots, standing on ground next to a dark object with white markings.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Bandaged lower legs and feet of a person standing outdoors.</div></div>","figure_shows":"This black-and-white photograph is cropped closely around people's lower legs and feet on rough ground. The central figure wears boots and overlapping bands of cloth wrapped around the calves; another booted leg is partly visible at the left. A rifle stands upright beside the central figure’s right boot, with pale markings on its stock. No faces, captions, arrows, or numbers are visible in this crop.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Caption/8","block_type":"Caption","html":"<hr/><p>Tanaka Shozo (1841-1913), the self-taught son of a farmer, rose to become a major political figure. He participated in the Popular Rights Movement in the 1880s, a movement demanding constitutional government. He was elected member to the first Diet. He believed that ordinary people should not be sacrificed for industrial progress. The Ashio Mine was polluting the Watarase river ruining 100 square miles of farmland and affecting a thousand families. The agitation forced the company to take pollution-control measures so that by 1904 harvests were normal.</p><hr/>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/Caption/9","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Young people being exhorted to fight for the nation: a magazine cover. Student-soldiers: photographs.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/8/PageFooter/10","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>‘Westernisation’ and ‘Tradition’</h3>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Successive generations of Japanese intellectuals had different views on Japan’s relations with other countries. To some, the USA and western European countries were at the highest point of civilisation, to which Japan aspired. Fukuzawa Yukichi, a leading Meiji intellectual, expressed this by saying that Japan must ‘expel Asia’. He meant that Japan must shed its ‘Asian’ characteristics and become part of the West.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4><b>Fukuzawa Yukichi (1835-1901)</b></h4>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Born in an impoverished samurai family, he studied in Nagasaki and Osaka learning Dutch and Western sciences and, later, English. In 1860, he went as a translator for the first Japanese embassy to the USA. This provided material for a book on the West, written not in the classical but in the spoken style that became extremely popular. He established a school that is today the Keio University. He was one of the core members of the Meirokusha, a society to promote Western learning.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>In <i>The Encouragement to Learning</i> (<i>Gakumon no susume</i>, 1872-76) he was very critical of Japanese knowledge: ‘All that Japan has to be proud of is its scenery’. He advocated not just modern factories and institutions but the cultural essence of the West – the spirit of civilisation. With this spirit it would be possible to build a new citizen. His principle was: ‘Heaven did not create men above men, nor set men below men.’</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The next generation questioned this total acceptance of Western ideas and urged that national pride be built on indigenous values. The philosopher Miyake Setsurei (1860-1945) argued that each nation must develop its special talents in the interest of world civilisation: ‘To devote oneself to one’s country is to devote oneself to the world.’ By contrast, many intellectuals were attracted to Western liberalism and wanted a Japan based not on the military but on democracy. Ueki Emori (1857-1892), a leader of the Popular Rights Movement, was demanding constitutional government, admired the French Revolution’s doctrine of the natural rights of man and of popular sovereignty, and spoke for a liberal education that would develop each individual: ‘Freedom is more precious than order.’ Others even advocated voting rights for women. This pressure led the government to announce a constitution.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/9/PageFooter/7","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>Daily Life</h4>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan's transformation into a modern society can be seen also in the changes in everyday life. The patriarchal household system comprised many generations living together under the control of the head of the house, but as more people became affluent, new ideas of the family spread. The new home (<i>homu</i> as the Japanese say, using the English word) was that of the nuclear family, where husband and wife lived as breadwinner and homemaker. This new concept of domesticity in turn generated demands for new types of domestic goods, new types of family entertainments, and new forms of housing. In the 1920s, construction companies made cheap</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/3","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.\" src=\"3668a836db39d25d24b56180a9c9a7fb_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-text\">of c<br/>p</p><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a rectangular metal grill with a wire mesh top, supported by four curved legs, with a coiled hose and small burner unit attached to the side.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The picture shows a rectangular, gold-coloured electric tabletop grill, with a wire cooking grid on top, raised legs, and dark handles at the sides. An electrical cord extends from the right side to a coiled lead and plug beside it. There are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers on the appliance; the image illustrates the electric grill mentioned in the caption.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/4","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>The novelty of electric goods: a rice-cooker, an American grill, a toaster.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/5","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.\" src=\"b5b16a821754dab72447d00e17ee376a_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure is a photograph of an antique sewing machine, showing a rectangular wooden cover with a woven-looking front panel above the work surface. To the right is an ornate open metal frame with curved patterns and an upper horizontal arm; two dark upright posts project above the machine. No people, arrows, numbers, or readable labels appear in the image.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>housing available for a down payment of 200 yen and a monthly instalment of 12 yen for ten years – this at a time when the salary of a bank employee (a person with higher education) was 40 yen per month.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/7","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>CAR-CLUB</h4>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Moga: An abbreviation for 'modern girl'. It represented the coming together in the twentieth century of ideas of gender equality, a cosmopolitan culture and a developed economy. The new middle-class families enjoyed new forms of travel and entertainment. Transport in cities improved with electric trams, public parks were opened from 1878, and department stores began to be built. In Tokyo, the Ginza became a fashionable area for <i>Ginbura</i>, a word combining 'Ginza' and 'burbura' (walking aimlessly). The first radio stations opened in 1925. Matsui Sumako, an actress, became a national star with her portrayal of Nora in the Norwegian writer Ibsen's <i>A Doll's House</i>. Movies began to be made in 1899 and soon there were a dozen companies making hundreds of films. The period was one of great vitality and the questioning of traditional norms of social and political behaviour.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/9","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.\" src=\"ea691955a06abea9018f39baa5ebe46d_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a group of women, some in traditional Japanese kimonos and others in Western-style coats and skirts, standing and crouching in front of a vintage automobile.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The sepia-toned photograph shows eight women posing in and around a small car outside a building. Five women stand beside the car, one sits in front, and two look out from inside; some wear patterned kimono, while others wear Western-style coats, skirts, and shoes. The car’s roof, side windows, and wheels are visible behind them. Captioned “Women’s car-pool,” the image depicts women sharing a car; there are no arrows, numbers, or explanatory labels.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/10","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Women's car-pool.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
