{"id":"/page/16/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>leaders such as Mahatma Gandhi and Kemal Ataturk, the modernist leader of Turkey. Its circulation increased rapidly from just 2,000 in 1926 to a massive 200,000 copies in 1933.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/SectionHeader/2","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4><p><b>ACTIVITY 4</b></p></h4>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>How does a sense of discrimination unite people?</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Shanghai in 1935: Buck Clayton, a black American trumpet player, in Shanghai with his jazz orchestra lived the life of the privileged expatriates. But he was black and once some white Americans assaulted him and his orchestra members and threw them out fro–m the hotel they played in. Thus, though American, he had greater sympathy for the plight of the Chinese being himself a victim of racial discrimination.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Of their fight with white Americans where they emerged victorious he writes, ‘The Chinese onlookers treated us like we had done something they always wanted to do and followed us all the way home cheering us like a winning football team.’</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>On the poverty and hard life of the Chinese, Clayton writes, ‘I would see sometimes twenty or thirty coolies pulling a big heavy cart that in America would be pulled by a truck or horses. These people seemed to be nothing but human horses and all they would get at the end of the day was just enough to get a couple of bowls of rice and a place to sleep. I don’t know how they did it.’</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Picture/7","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Artwork: Black and white woodcut style illustration of a man leaning over a table.\" src=\"f7bc9b0327ed4589a3faf9a7b3c92712_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-text\">LANCHIA</p><p class=\"img-desc\">A high-contrast black-and-white graphic print of a man with a focused expression leaning forward with his hands on a surface, signed 'LANCHIA' in the bottom right corner.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Artwork: Black and white woodcut style illustration of a man leaning over a table.</div></div>","figure_shows":"This stark black-and-white woodcut shows a rickshaw puller leaning steeply forward, his head at the upper right and his torso and arms filling most of the image. His hands grip two long rickshaw shafts running diagonally across the foreground, while heavy black shadows and angular white cuts emphasize his strained face and muscular effort. The rickshaw itself is outside the frame; there are no arrows, numbers or explanatory labels, and the artist’s signature, “LAN CHIA,” appears at the lower right. The image conveys the physical exertion of pulling a rickshaw.","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Caption/8","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>‘Rickshaw Puller’, woodcut by Lan Jia. The novel Rickshaw by Lao She (1936) became a classic.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/9","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The Guomindang despite its attempts to unite the country failed because of its narrow social base and limited political vision. A major plank in Sun Yat-sen’s programme – regulating capital and equalising land – was never carried out because the party ignored the peasantry and the rising social inequalities. It sought to impose military order rather then address the problems faced by the people.</p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
{"id":"/page/16/Text/10","block_type":"Text","html":"<p><i>The story of rising prices.</i></p>","taught":"NOT TAUGHT"}
