{"id":"/page/2/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h2>Introduction</h2>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>China and Japan present a marked physical contrast. China is a vast continental country that spans many climatic zones; the core is dominated by three major river systems: the Yellow River (Huang He), the Yangtse River (Chang Jiang – the third longest river in the world) and the Pearl River. A large part of the country is mountainous.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/Caption/3","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p>MAP 1: East Asia</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/Diagram/4","block_type":"Figure","html":"<img alt=\"Map of East Asia in 1941 showing Japanese territories. A red line outlines the extent of Japanese control, including Manchuria, North and South Korea, and the Japanese home islands (Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu). Major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Hiroshima, and Nagasaki are marked. Surrounding countries and bodies of water are labeled: Russia, Kazakhstan, Mongolia, China, India, Pacific Ocean, Sea of Japan/East Sea, Yellow Sea, and East China Sea.\" src=\"163688ca8da9787f5d48edd68d8cc75b_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-text\">RUSSIA</p><p class=\"img-text\">PACIFIC OCEAN</p><p class=\"img-text\">HOKKAIDO</p><p class=\"img-text\">MANCHURIA</p><p class=\"img-text\">HONSHU</p><p class=\"img-text\">SEA OF JAPAN /EAST SEA</p><p class=\"img-text\">JAPAN</p><p class=\"img-text\">KAZAKHISTAN</p><p class=\"img-text\">MONGOLIA</p><p class=\"img-text\">NORTH KOREA</p><p class=\"img-text\">Tokyo</p><p class=\"img-text\">SOUTH KOREA</p><p class=\"img-text\">Kyoto</p><p class=\"img-text\">SHIKOKU</p><p class=\"img-text\">Hiroshima</p><p class=\"img-text\">Nagasaki</p><p class=\"img-text\">KYUSHU</p><p class=\"img-text\">YELLOW SEA</p><p class=\"img-text\">Shanghai</p><p class=\"img-text\">EAST CHINA SEA</p><p class=\"img-text\">CHINA</p><p class=\"img-text\">JAPANESE TERRITORIES 1941</p><p class=\"img-text\">INDIA</p><p class=\"img-desc\">TAIWAN</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Map of East Asia in 1941 showing Japanese territories. A red line outlines the extent of Japanese control, including Manchuria, North and South Korea, and the Japanese home islands (Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, Kyushu). Major cities like Tokyo, Kyoto, Hiroshima, and Nagasaki are marked. Surrounding countries and bodies of water are labeled: Russia, Kazakhstan, Mongolia, China, India, Pacific Ocean, Sea of Japan/East Sea, Yellow Sea, and East China Sea.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The map of East Asia shows pale-yellow land and blue seas, with a red outline marking “Japanese territories 1941” across Japan, Korea, Manchuria, and parts of eastern China. Surrounding places are labeled Russia, Kazakhstan, Mongolia, China, and India; Japan’s islands are labeled Hokkaido, Honshu, Shikoku, and Kyushu, with Taiwan at the bottom edge. Red dots mark Tokyo, Kyoto, Hiroshima, Nagasaki, and Shanghai, and the water labels include the Pacific Ocean, Sea of Japan (East Sea), Yellow Sea, and East China Sea. The map illustrates the geographic extent of Japanese territories in 1941 relative to neighboring countries.","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The dominant ethnic group are the Han and the major language is Chinese (Putonghua) but there are many other nationalities, such as the Uighur, Hui, Manchu and Tibetan, and aside from dialects, such as Cantonese (Yue) and Shanghainese (Wu), there are other minority languages spoken as well.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Chinese food reflects this regional diversity with at least four distinct types. The best known is southern or Cantonese cuisine – as most overseas Chinese come from the Canton area – which includes dim sum (literally touch your heart), an assortment of pastries and dumplings. In the north, wheat is the staple food, while in Szechuan spices brought by Buddhist monks in the ancient period, along the silk route, and chillies by Portuguese traders in the fifteenth century, have created a fiery cuisine. In eastern China, both rice and wheat are eaten.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/2/PageFooter/7","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/3/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/3/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan, by contrast, is a string of islands, the four largest being Honshu, Kyushu, Shikoku and Hokkaido. The Okinawan chain is the southernmost, about the same latitude as the Bahamas. More than 50 per cent of the land area of the main islands is mountainous and Japan is situated in a very active earthquake zone. These geographical conditions have influenced architecture. The population is largely Japanese but there are a small Ainu minority and Koreans who were forcibly brought as labour when Korea was a Japanese colony.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan lacks a tradition of animal rearing. Rice is the staple crop and fish the major source of protein. Raw fish (sashimi or sushi) has now become a widely popular dish around the world as it is considered very healthy.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h2>JAPAN</h2>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/SectionHeader/4","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>The Political System</h3>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>An emperor had ruled Japan from Kyoto but by the twelfth century the imperial court lost power to shoguns, who in theory ruled in the name of the emperor. From 1603 to 1867, members of the Tokugawa family held the position of shogun. The country was divided into over 250 domains under the rule of lords called <i>daimyo</i>. The shogun exercised power over the domainal lords, ordering them to stay at the capital Edo (modern Tokyo) for long periods so that they would not pose a threat. He also controlled the major cities and mines. The samurai (the warrior class) were the ruling elite and served the shoguns and <i>daimyo</i>.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>In the late sixteenth century, three changes laid the pattern for future development. One, the peasantry was disarmed and only the samurai could carry swords. This ensured peace and order, ending the frequent wars of the previous century. Two, the <i>daimyo</i> were ordered to live in the capitals of their domains, each with a large degree of autonomy. Third, land surveys identified owners and taxpayers and graded land productivity to ensure a stable revenue base.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/Text/7","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The <i>daimyo</i>'s capitals became bigger, so that by the mid-seventeenth century, Japan not only had the most populated city in the world – Edo – but also two other large cities – Osaka and Kyoto, and at least half a dozen castle-towns with populations of over 50,000. (By contrast, most European countries of the time had only one large city.) This led to the growth of a commercial economy, and created financial and credit systems. A person's merit began to be more valued than his status. A vibrant culture blossomed in the towns, where the fast-growing class of merchants patronised theatre and the arts. As people enjoyed reading, it became possible for gifted writers to earn a living solely by writing. In Edo, people could 'rent' a book for the price of a bowl of noodles. This shows how popular reading had become and gives a glimpse into the scale of printing*.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/Footnote/8","block_type":"Footnote","html":"<p>* Printing was done with wood blocks. The Japanese did not like the regularity of European printing.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/3/PageFooter/9","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/4/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan was considered rich, because it imported luxury goods like silk from China and textiles from India. Paying for these imports with gold and silver strained the economy and led the Tokugawa to put restrictions on the export of precious metals. They also took steps to develop the silk industry in Nishijin in Kyoto so as to reduce imports. The silk from Nishijin came to be known as the best in the world. Other developments such as the increased use of money and the creation of a stock market in rice show that the economy was developing in new ways.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Social and intellectual changes – such as the study of ancient Japanese literature – led people to question the degree of Chinese influence and to argue that the essence of being Japanese could be found long before the contact with China, in such early classics as the <i>Tale of the Genji</i> and in the myths of origin that said that the islands were created by the gods and that the emperor was a descendant of the Sun Goddess.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>Tale of the Genji</h3>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>A fictionalised diary of the Heian court written by Murasaki Shikibu, the Tale of the Genji became the central work of fiction in Japanese literature. That period saw the emergence of many women writers, like Murasaki, who wrote in the Japanese script, while men wrote in the Chinese script, used for education and government. The novel depicts the romantic life of Prince Genji and is a striking picture of the aristocratic atmosphere of the Heian court. It shows the independence that women had in choosing their husbands and living their lives.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/SectionHeader/5","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>The Meiji Restoration</h3>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Internal discontent coincided with demands for trade and diplomatic relations. In 1853, the USA sent Commodore Matthew Perry (1794-1858) to Japan to demand that the government sign a treaty that would permit trade and open diplomatic relations, which it did the following year. Japan lay on the route to China which the USA saw as a major market; also, their whaling ships in the Pacific needed a place to refuel. At that time, there was only one Western country that traded with Japan, Holland.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/7","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Perry's arrival had an important effect on Japanese politics. The emperor, who till then had had little political power, now re-emerged as an important figure. In 1868, a movement forcibly removed the shogun from power, and brought the Emperor to Edo. This was made the capital and renamed Tokyo, which means 'eastern capital'.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<hr/><p>Nishijin is a quarter in Kyoto. In the sixteenth century, it had a weavers' guild of 31 households and by the end of the seventeenth century the community numbered over 70,000 people. Sericulture spread and was encouraged by an order in 1713 that only domestic yarn was to be used. Nishijin specialised only in the most expensive products. Silk production helped the growth of a class of regional entrepreneurs who challenged the Tokugawa order, and when foreign trade started in 1859 Japan's silk exports became a major source of profit for the economy struggling to compete with Western goods.</p><hr/>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/4/PageFooter/9","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/5/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/5/Caption/1","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Perry's ship:<br/>a Japanese woodblock<br/>print.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>What the Japanese called 'black ships' (tar was used to seal the joints of the wood) are depicted in paintings and cartoons showing the strange foreigners and their habits.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>This became a powerful symbol of Japan's 'opening'. (Today, scholars would argue that Japan had not been 'closed', took part in the east Asian trade and had access to knowledge of the wider world both through the Dutch and the Chinese.)</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Picture/4","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Illustration of a large, hybrid warship on the water. The ship has a traditional Japanese hull with a dragon head at the bow and a large, mechanical, steam-powered superstructure with three tall masts and a smokestack. A large, stylized face is on the front of the superstructure. The ship is sailing on a sea with waves. In the background, there are other ships and a flag. A text box in the upper right corner contains Japanese text describing the ship's dimensions and armaments.\" src=\"bdc6095967437c168a3f2a4ff8ca38bd_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">Illustration of a large, hybrid warship on the water. The ship has a traditional Japanese hull with a dragon head at the bow and a large, mechanical, steam-powered superstructure with three tall masts and a smokestack. A large, stylized face is on the front of the superstructure. The ship is sailing on a sea with waves. In the background, there are other ships and a flag. A text box in the upper right corner contains Japanese text describing the ship's dimensions and armaments.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The black-and-white woodblock print depicts a large steamship on choppy, curling waves, with three tall masts, extensive rigging, small pennants, and a large striped flag at the right. A thick plume of smoke rises behind the middle mast, and a large paddle wheel is visible on the side of the dark hull, which has rows of oval openings. The ship is fantastically stylized, with a long pointed projection and curling ornament at the left and an enormous face-like structure with staring eyes and teeth at the right; small figures appear on deck and among the rigging. A bordered panel of Japanese writing occupies the upper-right corner, presenting the ship through a highly decorative, dramatic Japanese depiction rather than a technical diagram.","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Officials and the people were aware that some European countries were building colonial empires in India and elsewhere. News of China being defeated by the British (see p. 166) was flowing in, and this was even depicted in popular plays, so that there was a real fear that Japan might be made a colony. Many scholars and leaders wanted to <i>learn</i> from the new ideas in Europe rather than ignore them as the Chinese were doing; others sought to exclude the Europeans even while being ready to adopt the new technologies they offered. Some argued for a gradual and limited 'opening' to the outer world.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The government launched a policy with the slogan '<i>fukoku kyohei</i>' (rich country, strong army). They realised that they needed to develop their economy and build a strong army, otherwise they would face the prospect of being subjugated like India. To do this they needed to create a sense of nationhood among the people, and to transform subjects into citizens.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/7","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>At the same time, the new government also worked to build what they called the 'emperor system'. (Japanese scholars use this term as the emperor was part of a system, along with the bureaucracy and the military, that exercised power.) Officials were sent to study the European monarchies on which they planned to model their own. The Emperor would be treated with reverence as he was considered a direct descendant of the Sun Goddess but he was also shown as the leader of westernisation. His birthday became a national holiday, he wore Western-style military uniforms, and edicts were issued in his name to set up modern institutions. The Imperial Rescript on Education of 1890 urged people to pursue learning, advance public good and promote common interests.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Picture/8","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Artwork: Traditional Chinese portrait painting of a bearded man with exaggerated facial features.\" src=\"c7b66d9f00cb74242a30209e8cedce6a_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white reproduction of a traditional Chinese portrait depicting a man with thick curly hair, a beard, and an exaggerated, wide nose, with vertical calligraphy in the upper right corner.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Artwork: Traditional Chinese portrait painting of a bearded man with exaggerated facial features.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure is a monochrome Japanese portrait of Commodore Perry, showing his head and part of his shoulders with tightly curled dark hair, thick eyebrows, a full beard and moustache, and an exaggeratedly large nose. His wide eyes, deeply creased forehead, and partly open mouth give him an intense expression; a decorated shoulder piece is visible at the lower right. Several vertical columns of Japanese writing appear in the upper-right corner. The portrait presents a stylized Japanese depiction of Perry rather than a naturalistic likeness.","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Caption/9","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Commodore Perry as<br/>seen by the Japanese.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/SectionHeader/10","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>ACTIVITY 1</h4>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/Text/11","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Contrast the encounter of the Japanese and the Aztecs with the Europeans.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/5/PageFooter/12","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/6/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>A new school system began to be built from the 1870s. Schooling was compulsory for boys and girls and by 1910 almost universal. Tuition fees were minimal. The curriculum had been based on Western models but by the 1870s, while emphasising modern ideas, stress was placed on loyalty and the study of Japanese history. The ministry of education exercised control over the curriculum and in the selection of textbooks, as well as in teachers' training. What was called 'moral culture' had to be taught, and texts urged children to revere their parents, be loyal to the nation, and become good citizens.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The Japanese had borrowed their written script from the Chinese in the sixth century. However, since their language is very different from Chinese they developed two phonetic alphabets – <i>hiragana</i> and <i>katakana</i>. <i>Hiragana</i> is considered feminine because it was used by many women writers in the Heian period (such as Murasaki). It is written using a mixture of Chinese characters and phonetics so that the main part of the word is written with a character – for instance, in 'going', 'go' would be written with a character and the 'ing' in phonetics.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The existence of a phonetic syllabary meant that knowledge spread from the elites to the wider society relatively quickly. In the 1880s it was suggested that Japanese develop a completely phonetic script, or adopt a European language. Neither was done.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>To integrate the nation, the Meiji government imposed a new administrative structure by altering old village and domain boundaries. The administrative unit had to have revenue adequate to maintain the local schools and health facilities, as well as serve as a recruitment centre for the military. All young men over twenty had to do a period of military service. A modern military force was developed. A legal system was set up to regulate the formation of political groups, control the holding of meetings and impose strict censorship. In all these measures the government had to face opposition. The military and the bureaucracy were put under the direct command of the emperor. This meant that even after a constitution was enacted these two groups remained outside the control of the government. In all these measures the government faced opposition.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The tension between these different ideals represented by a democratic constitution and a modern army was to have far-reaching consequences. The army pressed for a vigorous foreign policy to acquire more territory. This led to wars with China and Russia, in both of which Japan was the victor. Popular demand for greater democracy was often in opposition to the government's aggressive policies. Japan developed economically and acquired a colonial empire that suppressed the spread of democracy at home and put it in collision with the people it colonised.</p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/SectionHeader/6","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>大正デモクラシーとともに<p>こうした新聞の力が発揮されたのは大正デモクラシー運動</p></h4>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/Text/7","block_type":"Text","html":"<p><i>Writing Japanese: Kanji (Chinese characters) – red; katakana – blue; hiragana – green.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 1"}
{"id":"/page/6/PageFooter/8","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/7/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/7/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h2>Modernising the Economy</h2>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Another important part of the Meiji reforms was the modernising of the economy. Funds were raised by levying an agricultural tax. Japan's first railway line, between Tokyo and the port of Yokohama, was built in 1870-72. Textile machinery was imported from Europe, and foreign technicians were employed to train workers, as well as to teach in universities and schools, and Japanese students were sent abroad. In 1872, modern banking institutions were launched. Companies like Mitsubishi and Sumitomo were helped through subsidies and tax benefits to become major shipbuilders so that Japanese trade was from now on carried in Japanese ships. <i>Zaibatsu</i> (large business organisations controlled by individual families) dominated the economy till after the Second World War.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The population, 35 million in 1872, increased to 55 million in 1920. To reduce population pressure the government actively encouraged migration, first to the northern island of Hokkaido, which had been a largely autonomous area where the indigenous people called the Ainu lived, and then to Hawaii and Brazil, as well as to the growing colonial empire of Japan. Within Japan there was a shift to towns as industry developed. By 1925, 21 per cent of the population lived in cities; by 1935, this figure had gone up to 32 per cent (22.5 million).</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/SectionHeader/4","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>Industrial Workers</h3>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The number of people in manufacturing increased from 700,000 in 1870 to 4 million in 1913. Most of them worked in units employing less than five people and using neither machinery nor electric power.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Caption/6","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Workers in a textile factory.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Picture/7","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Interior of a large textile factory with rows of spinning machines and workers.\" src=\"f01b22e5bb303653828200595aece414_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a vast industrial textile mill with multiple rows of spinning frames loaded with spools of thread, supported by tall pillars, with two workers standing in the foreground.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Interior of a large textile factory with rows of spinning machines and workers.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The photograph shows a vast textile mill filled with tightly packed rows of spinning machines carrying numerous spools and vertical spindles, extending from the foreground into the distance. Tall pillars support the ceiling, with belts, shafts, and pulleys running overhead. Two workers stand with their backs to the camera beside the nearest machine, one at either side of the foreground. The repeated rows emphasize the scale of textile production and the machinery surrounding the workers; there are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Over half of those employed in modern factories were women. And it was women who organised the first modern strike in 1886. After 1900, the number of men began to increase but only in the 1930s did male workers begin to outnumber women.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/Text/9","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The size of factories also began to increase. Factories employing more than a hundred workers, just over 1,000 in 1909, jumped to over 2,000 by 1920 and 4,000 by the 1930s; yet even in 1940, there were over 550,000 workshops</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/7/PageFooter/10","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/8/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/1","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>that employed less than five employees. This sustained the family-centred ideology, just as nationalism was sustained by a strong patriarchal system under an emperor who was like a family patriarch.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The rapid and unregulated growth of industry and the demand for natural resources such as timber led to environmental destruction. Tanaka Shozo, elected to the first House of Representatives, launched the first agitation against industrial pollution in 1897 with 800 villagers in a mass protest forcing the government to take action.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>Aggressive Nationalism</h3>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The Meiji constitution was based on a restricted franchise and created a Diet (the Japanese used the German word for parliament because of the influence of German legal ideas) with limited powers. The leaders who brought about the imperial restoration continued to exercise power and even established political parties. Between 1918 and 1931, popularly elected prime ministers formed cabinets. Thereafter, they lost power to national unity cabinets formed across party lines. The emperor was the commander of the forces and from 1890 this was interpreted to mean that the army and the navy had independent control. In 1899, the prime minister ordered that only serving generals and admirals could become ministers. This strengthening of the military, together with the expansion of Japan's colonial empire, was connected with the fear that Japan was at the mercy of the Western powers. This fear was used to silence opposition to military expansion and to higher taxes to fund the armed forces.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/5","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Illustration of the cover of 'Shimin no Yu' (Friend of the Nation) magazine, showing three children in a row, arms outstretched, with the title '少國民の友' at the top.\" src=\"aaec7c7044d764c324cdb35c0201da6f_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">Illustration of the cover of 'Shimin no Yu' (Friend of the Nation) magazine, showing three children in a row, arms outstretched, with the title '少國民の友' at the top.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure shows a tilted, yellow-orange magazine cover with large red Japanese characters across the top and smaller dark characters on the right. Three young people, wearing headbands and with their upper bodies bare, stand in a staggered formation with their arms extended straight forward at shoulder height; the two in front wear dark trousers or shorts with belts. A broad blue-green band runs behind them across the middle of the cover. The coordinated pose presents young people participating in disciplined physical training, conveying a message of collective readiness.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/6","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Soldiers in uniform marching in a line carrying rifles.\" src=\"3e513f3d2635342934cf8ad226cfa879_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a line of uniformed soldiers marching forward on a wet, reflective surface, each holding a rifle upright.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Soldiers in uniform marching in a line carrying rifles.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The black-and-white photograph shows rows of young uniformed soldiers marching toward the camera on a wet, reflective street. They wear peaked caps and carry long rifles upright at their shoulders, with the barrels and bayonets extending above their heads. The repeated ranks and similar uniforms emphasize collective military discipline; there are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Picture/7","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: Bandaged lower legs and feet of a person standing outdoors.\" src=\"f5aaecb6473222e3d65f65164fab048e_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows the lower legs and feet of a person wrapped in multiple layers of bandages, wearing boots, standing on ground next to a dark object with white markings.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: Bandaged lower legs and feet of a person standing outdoors.</div></div>","figure_shows":"This black-and-white photograph is cropped closely around people's lower legs and feet on rough ground. The central figure wears boots and overlapping bands of cloth wrapped around the calves; another booted leg is partly visible at the left. A rifle stands upright beside the central figure’s right boot, with pale markings on its stock. No faces, captions, arrows, or numbers are visible in this crop.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Caption/8","block_type":"Caption","html":"<hr/><p>Tanaka Shozo (1841-1913), the self-taught son of a farmer, rose to become a major political figure. He participated in the Popular Rights Movement in the 1880s, a movement demanding constitutional government. He was elected member to the first Diet. He believed that ordinary people should not be sacrificed for industrial progress. The Ashio Mine was polluting the Watarase river ruining 100 square miles of farmland and affecting a thousand families. The agitation forced the company to take pollution-control measures so that by 1904 harvests were normal.</p><hr/>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/Caption/9","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Young people being exhorted to fight for the nation: a magazine cover. Student-soldiers: photographs.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/8/PageFooter/10","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/9/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>‘Westernisation’ and ‘Tradition’</h3>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Successive generations of Japanese intellectuals had different views on Japan’s relations with other countries. To some, the USA and western European countries were at the highest point of civilisation, to which Japan aspired. Fukuzawa Yukichi, a leading Meiji intellectual, expressed this by saying that Japan must ‘expel Asia’. He meant that Japan must shed its ‘Asian’ characteristics and become part of the West.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4><b>Fukuzawa Yukichi (1835-1901)</b></h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Born in an impoverished samurai family, he studied in Nagasaki and Osaka learning Dutch and Western sciences and, later, English. In 1860, he went as a translator for the first Japanese embassy to the USA. This provided material for a book on the West, written not in the classical but in the spoken style that became extremely popular. He established a school that is today the Keio University. He was one of the core members of the Meirokusha, a society to promote Western learning.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>In <i>The Encouragement to Learning</i> (<i>Gakumon no susume</i>, 1872-76) he was very critical of Japanese knowledge: ‘All that Japan has to be proud of is its scenery’. He advocated not just modern factories and institutions but the cultural essence of the West – the spirit of civilisation. With this spirit it would be possible to build a new citizen. His principle was: ‘Heaven did not create men above men, nor set men below men.’</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The next generation questioned this total acceptance of Western ideas and urged that national pride be built on indigenous values. The philosopher Miyake Setsurei (1860-1945) argued that each nation must develop its special talents in the interest of world civilisation: ‘To devote oneself to one’s country is to devote oneself to the world.’ By contrast, many intellectuals were attracted to Western liberalism and wanted a Japan based not on the military but on democracy. Ueki Emori (1857-1892), a leader of the Popular Rights Movement, was demanding constitutional government, admired the French Revolution’s doctrine of the natural rights of man and of popular sovereignty, and spoke for a liberal education that would develop each individual: ‘Freedom is more precious than order.’ Others even advocated voting rights for women. This pressure led the government to announce a constitution.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/PageFooter/7","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/10/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>Daily Life</h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan's transformation into a modern society can be seen also in the changes in everyday life. The patriarchal household system comprised many generations living together under the control of the head of the house, but as more people became affluent, new ideas of the family spread. The new home (<i>homu</i> as the Japanese say, using the English word) was that of the nuclear family, where husband and wife lived as breadwinner and homemaker. This new concept of domesticity in turn generated demands for new types of domestic goods, new types of family entertainments, and new forms of housing. In the 1920s, construction companies made cheap</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/3","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.\" src=\"3668a836db39d25d24b56180a9c9a7fb_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-text\">of c<br/>p</p><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a rectangular metal grill with a wire mesh top, supported by four curved legs, with a coiled hose and small burner unit attached to the side.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The picture shows a rectangular, gold-coloured electric tabletop grill, with a wire cooking grid on top, raised legs, and dark handles at the sides. An electrical cord extends from the right side to a coiled lead and plug beside it. There are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers on the appliance; the image illustrates the electric grill mentioned in the caption.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/4","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>The novelty of electric goods: a rice-cooker, an American grill, a toaster.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/5","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.\" src=\"b5b16a821754dab72447d00e17ee376a_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure is a photograph of an antique sewing machine, showing a rectangular wooden cover with a woven-looking front panel above the work surface. To the right is an ornate open metal frame with curved patterns and an upper horizontal arm; two dark upright posts project above the machine. No people, arrows, numbers, or readable labels appear in the image.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>housing available for a down payment of 200 yen and a monthly instalment of 12 yen for ten years – this at a time when the salary of a bank employee (a person with higher education) was 40 yen per month.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/7","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>CAR-CLUB</h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Moga: An abbreviation for 'modern girl'. It represented the coming together in the twentieth century of ideas of gender equality, a cosmopolitan culture and a developed economy. The new middle-class families enjoyed new forms of travel and entertainment. Transport in cities improved with electric trams, public parks were opened from 1878, and department stores began to be built. In Tokyo, the Ginza became a fashionable area for <i>Ginbura</i>, a word combining 'Ginza' and 'burbura' (walking aimlessly). The first radio stations opened in 1925. Matsui Sumako, an actress, became a national star with her portrayal of Nora in the Norwegian writer Ibsen's <i>A Doll's House</i>. Movies began to be made in 1899 and soon there were a dozen companies making hundreds of films. The period was one of great vitality and the questioning of traditional norms of social and political behaviour.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/9","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.\" src=\"ea691955a06abea9018f39baa5ebe46d_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a group of women, some in traditional Japanese kimonos and others in Western-style coats and skirts, standing and crouching in front of a vintage automobile.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The sepia-toned photograph shows eight women posing in and around a small car outside a building. Five women stand beside the car, one sits in front, and two look out from inside; some wear patterned kimono, while others wear Western-style coats, skirts, and shoes. The car’s roof, side windows, and wheels are visible behind them. Captioned “Women’s car-pool,” the image depicts women sharing a car; there are no arrows, numbers, or explanatory labels.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/10","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Women's car-pool.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/PageFooter/11","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/11/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/11/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h2>‘Overcoming Modernity’</h2>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>State-centred nationalism found full expression in the 1930s and 1940s as Japan launched wars to extend its empire in China and other parts of Asia, a war that merged into the Second World War after Japan attacked the USA at Pearl Harbor. This period saw greater controls on society, the repression and imprisonment of dissidents, as well as the formation of patriotic societies, many of them women’s organisations, to support the war.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/Text/3","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>An influential symposium on ‘Overcoming Modernity’ in 1943 debated the dilemma facing Japan – of how to combat the West while being modern. A musician, Moroi Saburo, posed the question of how to rescue music from the art of sensory stimulation and restore it to an art of the spirit. He was not rejecting Western music but trying to find a way that went beyond merely rewriting or playing Japanese music on Western instruments. The philosopher Nishitani Keiji defined ‘modern’ as the unity of three streams of Western thought: the Renaissance, the Protestant Reformation, and the rise of natural sciences. He argued that Japan’s ‘moral energy’ (a term taken from the German philosopher Ranke) had helped it to escape colonisation and it was its duty to establish a new world order, a Greater East Asia. For this a new vision that would integrate science and religion was necessary.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/SectionHeader/4","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>ACTIVITY 2</h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Would you agree with Nishitani’s definition of ‘modern’?</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/SectionHeader/6","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>After Defeat: Re-emerging as a Global Economic Power</h3>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/Text/7","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan’s attempt to carve out a colonial empire ended with its defeat by the Allied forces. It has been argued that nuclear bombs were dropped on Hiroshima and Nagasaki to shorten the war. But others think the immense destruction and suffering it caused were unnecessary. Under the US-led Occupation (1945-47) Japan was demilitarised and a new constitution introduced. This had Article 9, the so-called ‘no war clause’ that renounces the use of war as an instrument of state policy. Agrarian reforms, the re-establishment of trade unions and an attempt to dismantle the <i>zaibatsu</i> or large monopoly houses that dominated the Japanese economy were also carried out. Political parties were revived and the first post-war elections held in 1946 where women voted for the first time.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/11/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The rapid rebuilding of the Japanese economy after its shattering defeat was called a post-war ‘miracle’. But it was more than that – it was firmly rooted in its long history. The constitution was democratised only now, but the Japanese had a historic tradition of popular struggles and intellectual engagement with how to broaden political participation. The social cohesion of the pre-war years was strengthened, allowing for a close working of the government, bureaucracy and industry. US support, as well as the demand created by the Korean and the Vietnamese wars also helped the Japanese economy.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
