{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h3>‘Westernisation’ and ‘Tradition’</h3>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Successive generations of Japanese intellectuals had different views on Japan’s relations with other countries. To some, the USA and western European countries were at the highest point of civilisation, to which Japan aspired. Fukuzawa Yukichi, a leading Meiji intellectual, expressed this by saying that Japan must ‘expel Asia’. He meant that Japan must shed its ‘Asian’ characteristics and become part of the West.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/SectionHeader/3","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4><b>Fukuzawa Yukichi (1835-1901)</b></h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/4","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Born in an impoverished samurai family, he studied in Nagasaki and Osaka learning Dutch and Western sciences and, later, English. In 1860, he went as a translator for the first Japanese embassy to the USA. This provided material for a book on the West, written not in the classical but in the spoken style that became extremely popular. He established a school that is today the Keio University. He was one of the core members of the Meirokusha, a society to promote Western learning.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/5","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>In <i>The Encouragement to Learning</i> (<i>Gakumon no susume</i>, 1872-76) he was very critical of Japanese knowledge: ‘All that Japan has to be proud of is its scenery’. He advocated not just modern factories and institutions but the cultural essence of the West – the spirit of civilisation. With this spirit it would be possible to build a new citizen. His principle was: ‘Heaven did not create men above men, nor set men below men.’</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>The next generation questioned this total acceptance of Western ideas and urged that national pride be built on indigenous values. The philosopher Miyake Setsurei (1860-1945) argued that each nation must develop its special talents in the interest of world civilisation: ‘To devote oneself to one’s country is to devote oneself to the world.’ By contrast, many intellectuals were attracted to Western liberalism and wanted a Japan based not on the military but on democracy. Ueki Emori (1857-1892), a leader of the Popular Rights Movement, was demanding constitutional government, admired the French Revolution’s doctrine of the natural rights of man and of popular sovereignty, and spoke for a liberal education that would develop each individual: ‘Freedom is more precious than order.’ Others even advocated voting rights for women. This pressure led the government to announce a constitution.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/9/PageFooter/7","block_type":"PageFooter","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/10/PageHeader/0","block_type":"PageHeader","html":"","taught":"skipped"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/1","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>Daily Life</h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/2","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Japan's transformation into a modern society can be seen also in the changes in everyday life. The patriarchal household system comprised many generations living together under the control of the head of the house, but as more people became affluent, new ideas of the family spread. The new home (<i>homu</i> as the Japanese say, using the English word) was that of the nuclear family, where husband and wife lived as breadwinner and homemaker. This new concept of domesticity in turn generated demands for new types of domestic goods, new types of family entertainments, and new forms of housing. In the 1920s, construction companies made cheap</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/3","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.\" src=\"3668a836db39d25d24b56180a9c9a7fb_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-text\">of c<br/>p</p><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a rectangular metal grill with a wire mesh top, supported by four curved legs, with a coiled hose and small burner unit attached to the side.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A portable metal charcoal grill with a coiled hose and burner attachment.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The picture shows a rectangular, gold-coloured electric tabletop grill, with a wire cooking grid on top, raised legs, and dark handles at the sides. An electrical cord extends from the right side to a coiled lead and plug beside it. There are no visible labels, arrows, or numbers on the appliance; the image illustrates the electric grill mentioned in the caption.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/4","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>The novelty of electric goods: a rice-cooker, an American grill, a toaster.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/5","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.\" src=\"b5b16a821754dab72447d00e17ee376a_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><div class=\"img-alt\">A wooden hand-cranked mangle or press used for flattening paper or cloth.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The figure is a photograph of an antique sewing machine, showing a rectangular wooden cover with a woven-looking front panel above the work surface. To the right is an ornate open metal frame with curved patterns and an upper horizontal arm; two dark upright posts project above the machine. No people, arrows, numbers, or readable labels appear in the image.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/6","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>housing available for a down payment of 200 yen and a monthly instalment of 12 yen for ten years – this at a time when the salary of a bank employee (a person with higher education) was 40 yen per month.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/SectionHeader/7","block_type":"SectionHeader","html":"<h4>CAR-CLUB</h4>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Text/8","block_type":"Text","html":"<p>Moga: An abbreviation for 'modern girl'. It represented the coming together in the twentieth century of ideas of gender equality, a cosmopolitan culture and a developed economy. The new middle-class families enjoyed new forms of travel and entertainment. Transport in cities improved with electric trams, public parks were opened from 1878, and department stores began to be built. In Tokyo, the Ginza became a fashionable area for <i>Ginbura</i>, a word combining 'Ginza' and 'burbura' (walking aimlessly). The first radio stations opened in 1925. Matsui Sumako, an actress, became a national star with her portrayal of Nora in the Norwegian writer Ibsen's <i>A Doll's House</i>. Movies began to be made in 1899 and soon there were a dozen companies making hundreds of films. The period was one of great vitality and the questioning of traditional norms of social and political behaviour.</p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Picture/9","block_type":"Picture","html":"<img alt=\"Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.\" src=\"ea691955a06abea9018f39baa5ebe46d_img.jpg\"/><div class=\"img-description\" style=\"border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;\"><p class=\"img-desc\">A black-and-white photograph shows a group of women, some in traditional Japanese kimonos and others in Western-style coats and skirts, standing and crouching in front of a vintage automobile.</p><div class=\"img-alt\">Photo: A group of young women posing in front of a vintage car.</div></div>","figure_shows":"The sepia-toned photograph shows eight women posing in and around a small car outside a building. Five women stand beside the car, one sits in front, and two look out from inside; some wear patterned kimono, while others wear Western-style coats, skirts, and shoes. The car’s roof, side windows, and wheels are visible behind them. Captioned “Women’s car-pool,” the image depicts women sharing a car; there are no arrows, numbers, or explanatory labels.","taught":"in lecture 2"}
{"id":"/page/10/Caption/10","block_type":"Caption","html":"<p><i>Women's car-pool.</i></p>","taught":"in lecture 2"}
